<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1940 CE – 1990 CE · capital Vilnius</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet Lithuania, created by the annexation of 1940, which became the first Soviet republic to declare independence in 1990.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Under the secret protocols of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania</a> in June 1940. A rigged election produced a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-seimas">People's Seimas</a> that requested admission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> was annexed in August 1940, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, regained in 1939, as its capital.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soviet rule brought nationalisation, collectivisation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> in 1941 and after the war. German occupation in 1941-1944 saw the murder of most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. After 1944 armed partisans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forest-brothers">Forest Brothers</a>, resisted until the early 1950s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antanas-snieckus">Antanas Sniečkus</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party</a> from 1940 to 1974, and the republic industrialised while Catholic dissent remained strong.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1988 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sajudis">Sąjūdis</a> reform movement emerged, and in 1989 the Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas-brazauskas">Algirdas Brazauskas</a> broke from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. On 11 March 1990 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-council-of-the-republic-of-lithuania">Supreme Council</a> declared the restoration of the independent <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-lithuania">Republic of Lithuania</a>, recognised internationally in 1991.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lithuanian-soviet-socialist-republic.jpg" alt="Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Lithuanian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1953%E2%80%931988).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1940 CE – 1990 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vilnius</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antanas Sniečkus, Algirdas Brazauskas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (state atheism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Lithuanian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lithuanian SSR, Soviet Lithuania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1965&amp;focus=lithuanian-soviet-socialist-republic" aria-label="Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5236 306 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lithuanian Soviet Socialist Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5556 562L5526 548M5556 562L5820 533M5556 562L5526 556"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5526 548h0M5820 533h0M5526 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5556 562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vilnius. <a href="/atlas?year=1965&amp;focus=lithuanian-soviet-socialist-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
